{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f4cbfc58a683894a66b028420969bb75a35fe4acda1bd3dce0024062c11b2041", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " \"The following are the opening statements of a news article from the Guardian.\n\nA unique version of Sudoku is introduced in this article. The typical regulations of Sudoku still apply, but there are some extra rules. Cells enclosed by gray lines must contain numbers that add up to the numbers in the corners of the shape. No digit can be duplicated inside each shape delineated by gray lines. To view the finished puzzle, get next week’s edition of the Observer Digital Edition.\n\nThis Sudoku version presents a new challenge to players, as they must consider the sum of the numbers in the grey-lined shapes in addition to the regular Sudoku rules. It is a great way to exercise the brain and enhance problem-solving skills.\n\nAddition rules are applied to the grey-lined shapes, which adds a new layer of complexity to the game. The objective of the game remains the same: to fill in the grid with numbers from 1 to 9, without repeating any digit in a row, column, or within the grey-lined shapes.\n\nPlayers must be extra careful to ensure that the sum of the numbers in the grey-lined shapes matches the figures in the corners. This may require strategic placement of numbers and careful planning ahead. The game's difficulty level increases as the size of the grey-lined shapes grows.\n\nSolving this Sudoku variant requires attention to detail and the ability to think several steps ahead. Players can use different strategies, such as cross-hatching or guess and check, to fill in the grid while adhering to the new rules. The completed puzzle will be available in the following week’s digital edition of the Observer.\"", "logprobs": [{"token": "\"", "logprob": -0.0005287918029353023}, {"token": "The", "logprob": -0.6523257493972778}, {"token": "following", "logprob": -0.09